#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:9
msgid "Creating a simple PDE model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:20
msgid ""
"In the `previous notebook <./1-an-ode-model.ipynb>`__ we show how to create, "
"discretise and solve an ODE model in pybamm. In this notebook we show how to "
"create and solve a PDE problem, which will require meshing of the spatial "
"domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:22
msgid ""
"As an example, we consider the problem of linear diffusion on a unit sphere,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:24
msgid "\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t} = \\nabla \\cdot (\\nabla c),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:29
msgid "with the following boundary and initial conditions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:31
msgid ""
"\\left.\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial r}\\right\\vert_{r=0} = 0, \\quad "
"\\left.\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial r}\\right\\vert_{r=1} = 2, \\quad "
"\\left.c\\right\\vert_{t=0} = 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:36
msgid ""
"As before, we begin by importing the PyBaMM library into this notebook, "
"along with any other packages we require:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[1]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"%pip install \"pybamm[plot,cite]\" -q    # install PyBaMM if it is not "
"installed\n"
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"import numpy as np\n"
"\n"
"import pybamm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:61
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:67
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:74
msgid "Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:86
msgid "Setting up the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:97
msgid ""
"As in the previous example, we start with a ``pybamm.BaseModel`` object and "
"define our model variables. Since we are now solving a PDE we need to tell "
"pybamm the domain each variable belongs to so that it can be discretised in "
"space in the correct way. This is done by passing the keyword argument "
"``domain``, and in this example we choose the domain \"negative particle\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model = pybamm.BaseModel()\n"
"\n"
"c = pybamm.Variable(\"Concentration\", domain=\"negative particle\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:120
msgid ""
"Note that we have given our variable the (useful) name \"Concentration\", "
"but the symbol representing this variable is simply ``c``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:131
msgid ""
"We then state out governing equations. Sometime it is useful to define "
"intermediate quantities in order to express the governing equations more "
"easily. In this example we define the flux, then define the rhs to be minus "
"the divergence of the flux. The equation is then added to the dictionary "
"``model.rhs``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"N = -pybamm.grad(c)  # define the flux\n"
"dcdt = -pybamm.div(N)  # define the rhs equation\n"
"\n"
"model.rhs = {c: dcdt}  # add the equation to rhs dictionary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:155
msgid ""
"Unlike ODE models, PDE models require both initial and boundary conditions. "
"Similar to initial conditions, boundary conditions can be added using the "
"dictionary ``model.boundary_conditions``. Boundary conditions for each "
"variable are provided as a dictionary of the form ``{side: (value, type)``, "
"where, in 1D, side can be \"left\" or \"right\", value is the value of the "
"boundary conditions, and type is the type of boundary condition (at present, "
"this can be \"Dirichlet\" or \"Neumann\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# initial conditions\n"
"model.initial_conditions = {c: pybamm.Scalar(1)}\n"
"\n"
"# boundary conditions\n"
"lbc = pybamm.Scalar(0)\n"
"rbc = pybamm.Scalar(2)\n"
"model.boundary_conditions = {c: {\"left\": (lbc, \"Neumann\"), \"right\": "
"(rbc, \"Neumann\")}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:182
msgid ""
"Note that in our example the boundary conditions take constant values, but "
"the value can be any valid pybamm expression."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:184
msgid ""
"Finally, we add any variables of interest to the dictionary ``model."
"variables``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "model.variables = {\"Concentration\": c, \"Flux\": N}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:206
msgid "Using the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:217
msgid ""
"Now the model is now completely defined all that remains is to discretise "
"and solve. Since this model is a PDE we need to define the geometry on which "
"it will be solved, and choose how to mesh the geometry and discretise in "
"space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:229
msgid "Defining a geometry and mesh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:231
msgid ""
"We can define spatial variables in a similar way to how we defined model "
"variables, providing a domain and a coordinate system. The geometry on which "
"we wish to solve the model is defined using a nested dictionary. The first "
"key is the domain name (here \"negative particle\") and the entry is a "
"dictionary giving the limits of the domain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# define geometry\n"
"r = pybamm.SpatialVariable(\n"
"    \"r\", domain=[\"negative particle\"], coord_sys=\"spherical polar\"\n"
")\n"
"geometry = {\n"
"    \"negative particle\": {r: {\"min\": pybamm.Scalar(0), \"max\": pybamm."
"Scalar(1)}}\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:258
msgid "We then create a uniform one-dimensional mesh with 20 points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[7]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# mesh and discretise\n"
"submesh_types = {\"negative particle\": pybamm.Uniform1DSubMesh}\n"
"var_pts = {r: 20}\n"
"mesh = pybamm.Mesh(geometry, submesh_types, var_pts)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:282
msgid ""
"Example of meshes that do require parameters include the ``pybamm."
"Exponential1DSubMesh`` which clusters points close to one or both boundaries "
"using an exponential rule. It takes a parameter which sets how closely the "
"points are clustered together, and also lets the users select the side on "
"which more points should be clustered. For example, to create a mesh with "
"more nodes clustered to the right (i.e. the surface in the particle "
"problem), using a stretch factor of 2, we pass an instance of the "
"exponential submesh class and a dictionary of parameters into the "
"``MeshGenerator`` class as follows: ``pybamm.MeshGenerator(pybamm."
"Exponential1DSubMesh, submesh_params={\"side\": \"right\", \"stretch\": 2})``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:294
msgid ""
"After defining a mesh we choose a spatial method. Here we choose the Finite "
"Volume Method. We then set up a discretisation by passing the mesh and "
"spatial methods to the class ``pybamm.Discretisation``. The model is then "
"processed, turning the variables into (slices of) a statevector, spatial "
"variables into vector and spatial operators into matrix-vector "
"multiplications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[8]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"spatial_methods = {\"negative particle\": pybamm.FiniteVolume()}\n"
"disc = pybamm.Discretisation(mesh, spatial_methods)\n"
"disc.process_model(model);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:317
msgid "Now that the model has been discretised we are ready to solve."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:329
msgid "Solving the model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:340
msgid ""
"As before, we choose a solver and times at which we want the solution "
"returned. We then solve, extract the variables we are interested in, and "
"plot the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid "[9]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"# solve\n"
"solver = pybamm.IDAKLUSolver()\n"
"t = [0, 1]\n"
"solution = solver.solve(model, t)\n"
"\n"
"# post-process, so that the solution can be called at any time t or space r\n"
"# (using interpolation)\n"
"c = solution[\"Concentration\"]\n"
"\n"
"# plot\n"
"fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 4))\n"
"\n"
"ax1.plot(solution.t, c(solution.t, r=1))\n"
"ax1.set_xlabel(\"t\")\n"
"ax1.set_ylabel(\"Surface concentration\")\n"
"r = np.linspace(0, 1, 100)\n"
"ax2.plot(r, c(t=0.5, r=r))\n"
"ax2.set_xlabel(\"r\")\n"
"ax2.set_ylabel(\"Concentration at t=0.5\")\n"
"plt.tight_layout()\n"
"plt.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/creating_models/2-a-pde-model.ipynb:389
msgid ""
"In the `next notebook <./3-negative-particle-problem.ipynb>`__ we build on "
"the example here to to solve the problem of diffusion in the negative "
"electrode particle within the single particle model. In doing so we will "
"also cover how to include parameters in a model."
msgstr ""

